OL,3,GNEWSADrought leaves 'barely any apples'
OL,4,Ato make cider
OL,5,
OL,6,FSomerset's famous cider orchards have
OL,7,Fbeen 'decimated' by the long summer
OL,8,Fdrought, producers say. Many trees have
OL,9,Fproduced no fruit at all, and others
OL,10,Fhave dropped their fruit early, rotting
OL,11,Fbefore they ripened.
OL,13,F"It's gut-wrenching," says Kimberly
OL,14,FJones of Honey's Cider near Bath, whose
OL,15,Forchard has produced only a 10th of its
OL,16,Fnormal amount of apples.
OL,18,FNeil MacDonald, who manages more than
OL,19,F1,000 acres of Somerset orchards, says
OL,20,Fhe has watched half his apples fall
OL,21,Fbefore they have ripened, because the
OL,22,Ftrees are stressed by the drought.
OL,23,APub: 25-AugQppppppppppppppppppppppG1/2
OL,24,ANext PageBW-SW Eng NewsC Reg TVFWeather
